::STAT_U05_L06_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L06_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.5
Question:
Two random samples compare average study time in two schools. What conclusion is being supported?
{
~A. A treatment effect from random assignment#No treatment was assigned, so this is not a treatment effect.
~B. A census of every student#A census would include everyone.
=C. An inference about two populations#Random samples support inference about the populations.
~D. A counting permutation#Permutation is unrelated to population inference.
}

::STAT_U05_L06_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L06_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.6
Question:
Students are randomly assigned to Method A or Method B. What does random assignment help support?
{
~A. A biased volunteer sample#Volunteer bias is not random assignment.
~B. A conclusion about every population without caution#Conclusions still require context and caution.
~C. A margin of error only#Margin of error is not the main purpose of random assignment.
=D. A fair comparison of treatment effects#Random assignment helps make treatment groups comparable.
}

::STAT_U05_L06_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L06_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.5
Question:
Why should two samples be random when comparing populations?
{
~A. It removes all variation#Sampling variation still exists.
=B. Random sampling helps each sample represent its population#Representativeness supports population inference.
~C. It guarantees identical sample means#Random samples can still have different means.
~D. It changes the populations#Sampling does not change the populations.
}

::STAT_U05_L06_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L06_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.6
Question:
Which situation is a randomized experiment?
{
=A. Participants are randomly assigned to two treatments#Random treatment assignment defines a randomized experiment.
~B. A survey asks opinions about treatment#A survey asks questions but does not assign treatment.
~C. Participants choose their own treatment#Self-selection can create confounding.
~D. Only past records are observed#Observing past records is observational.
}

::STAT_U05_L06_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U05_L06_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.INF.5
Question:
In a class data check, two random samples compare average study time in two schools. What conclusion is being supported?
{
=A. An inference about two populations#Random samples support inference about the populations.
~B. A treatment effect from random assignment#No treatment was assigned, so this is not a treatment effect.
~C. A census of every student#A census would include everyone.
~D. A counting permutation#Permutation is unrelated to population inference.
}
